---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
/* Copy approved by Peter, 2026-11-19. Styled like /for-agents. */
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Why doesn’t my AI write like me? | Milton"
  description="What helps without Milton, where Milton fits, and its limits."
>
  <article class="briefing">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Why doesn’t my AI write like me?</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Because it hasn’t seen how you write. Without your writing in front of
        it, an AI falls back on its defaults, and the strongest one is
        formality. Describing your style doesn’t fix that for long: “casual and
        direct” is a mood, and your actual sentences carry habits you can’t
        name.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-helps">
      <h2 id="what-helps">What helps, without Milton</h2>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Paste what you actually sent.</strong> A few pieces of the
          same kind as what you’re writing, on different topics, exactly as
          sent, typos included.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tell it what to drop.</strong> Formality is the default most
          worth overriding. Say it plainly: “Use contractions. No greeting line.
          Three sentences.”
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Show it your fixes.</strong> When you rewrite its draft, paste
          your version back. What you changed says more than any description.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep it where your AI will read it.</strong> Custom
          instructions, a project, or a Markdown file you paste in. If you keep
          that file current and your AI reads it every time, that’s enough.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="why-it-slips">
      <h2 id="why-it-slips">Why it still slips</h2>
      <ul class="instruction-list">
        <li>
          Every new chat starts from the defaults unless something puts your
          writing back in front of it.
        </li>
        <li>
          One profile for everything blurs a Slack reply and an investor update
          into one voice.
        </li>
        <li>Files go stale. Nobody updates one after every email.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="where-milton-fits">
      <h2 id="where-milton-fits">Where Milton fits</h2>
      <p>
        Milton does the keeping. When you accept a final, your AI keeps it as a
        memo: its first draft and what you sent. Milton’s code works out what
        you changed. Before your AI drafts, it reads the finals closest to what
        it’s writing, with your changes.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it can’t do: it hasn’t been shown to write better than the same
        examples kept in a Markdown file, and we haven’t measured whether it
        cuts your edits. Your AI still does the writing, and it has to call
        Milton to use it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="action-row closing-row">
      <a class="button" href="https://app.meetmilton.com/login">
        Try Milton free <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/research">How we tested</a>
      <a href="/for-agents">For agents</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>

<style>
  .briefing {
    padding-block: var(--space-16);
  }
  .page-head {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }
  h1 {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-page);
  }
  .lead {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  section {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-title);
  }
  p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  p + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  p,
  li {
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* The button, then two text links at touch height. */
  .closing-row {
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3) var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .closing-row > a:not(.button) {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--touch-target);
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .briefing {
      padding-block: var(--space-10);
    }
  }
</style>
